### Audit events — /audit-events
#
# Read-only by design. There is no endpoint that writes, edits or deletes an entry, and the entries
# themselves are written inside the transaction of the change they describe — so a loan exists if
# and only if the trail records it.
#
# Refusals are not here. The trail says what the library did; an attempt that changed no state is a
# log line, and it is logged.

### Read the audit trail — 200, newest first.
GET {{host}}/audit-events?pageNumber=1&pageSize=20
X-Actor: {{actor}}

### Everything that ever happened to one book — 200.
GET {{host}}/audit-events?entityType=book&entityId={{bookId}}
X-Actor: {{actor}}

### Every loan the library made — 200.
# The action names are stable strings rather than enum numbers, because the trail is read by people
# and by tools that never compile against this codebase.
GET {{host}}/audit-events?action=loan.created
X-Actor: {{actor}}

### Everything one actor did — 200.
GET {{host}}/audit-events?actor={{actor}}
X-Actor: {{actor}}

### Write something under a correlation identifier of your own, then trace it.
# The API echoes X-Correlation-Id on every response and stamps it on every entry the request
# produces. Supplying one here means the next request can pull the whole thing back out.
#
# A PATCH rather than a POST deliberately: it can be run repeatedly. A create would answer 409 on
# the second run, and a refused request writes no audit entry — so the trace below would come back
# empty and look like a broken filter when it is the trail correctly recording that nothing
# happened.
PATCH {{host}}/books/{{bookId}}
X-Actor: {{actor}}
X-Correlation-Id: {{correlationId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "author": "Martin Fowler, with Kent Beck"
}

### Trace that one request end to end — 200.
# The filter that turns the trail from a list into an explanation: everything one request caused,
# across every entity it touched.
GET {{host}}/audit-events?correlationId={{correlationId}}
X-Actor: {{actor}}

### An unknown entity type — 400.
# Refused rather than answered with an empty page, which is exactly the wrong answer to give
# somebody investigating an incident.
GET {{host}}/audit-events?entityType=spaceship
X-Actor: {{actor}}
